    "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/MiniMax-AI/OpenAgentCore/tarball/v0.0.12-rc.2",
    "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/MiniMax-AI/OpenAgentCore/zipball/v0.0.12-rc.2",
    "body": "OpenAgentCore v0.0.12-rc.2\n\nPrerelease for testing installs, upgrades and uninstalls. `latest` still points to v0.0.11.\n\nInstall, or upgrade an installation of v0.0.10 or later after a backup ([Upgrade](https://github.com/MiniMax-AI/OpenAgentCore/blob/main/docs/getting-started/operations.md#upgrade)):\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsSL https://github.com/MiniMax-AI/OpenAgentCore/releases/download/v0.0.12-rc.2/install.sh | bash -s -- --version v0.0.12-rc.2\ncurl -fsSL https://github.com/MiniMax-AI/OpenAgentCore/releases/download/v0.0.12-rc.2/install.sh | bash -s -- --version v0.0.12-rc.2 --upgrade --install-dir \"$HOME/.oac/core\"\n```\n\n**Protocol changes:** none since v0.0.10. Nodes, the microsandbox helper, E2B templates and self-hosted machines keep working; applying this release's Runtime is optional.\n\n**Operator notes:**\n- PostgreSQL now comes from `ghcr.io/minimax-ai/openagentcore/postgres`, the official `postgres:16-alpine` image pinned by digest, so installs no longer need Docker Hub. An upgrade switches to it with no data change.\n- Node enrollment commands are valid for 1 hour.\n- A node keeps the proxy settings its install command ran with, in `/etc/oac-node/<installation-id>.env`.\n\nChanges since v0.0.12-rc.1:\n\n- **Uninstall.** `oac uninstall` removes a Core installation: containers, data volume, directory and lock, after a confirmation. `oac-daemon uninstall` removes a self-hosted machine's installation and keeps its workspace. A node's **Clean up a host** command also deletes its microsandbox store and the account the installer created.\n- **Recover from a failed or interrupted run.** Ctrl-C at any step says to rerun the same command, and the rerun continues. Core, node and self-hosted downloads resume where they stopped. A node install that never registered recovers on rerun.\n- **Clear failures.** The installers check Docker access, the Web port and the public URL before they change anything, and name the fix: the docker group, the installation that holds the port, or a root-owned path. A node that cannot connect logs why.\n- **Safer by default.** Installation grants stay out of process arguments; self-hosted uninstall deletes only what its installation owns; the Core key is printed only on a fresh install, and reruns print `oac core-key --show`.\n"
  },
